16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"The Competition Reality"}],["$","span","1",{"children":": QuestMobile data shows Kimi's monthly active users dropped from 21.65 million to 9.02 million by late 2025, while Doubao, Qwen, Yuanbao, and DeepSeek all exceeded 100 million MAU."}]]}],["$","p","197",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"The \"80/20 Rule\""}],["$","span","1",{"children":": Industry consolidation is concentrating 80% of traffic in 20% of applications. Kimi faces an existential question: can it maintain independent trajectory, or does it need public market capital to stay competitive?"}]]}],["$","h3","199",{"style":{"fontSize":"22px","fontWeight":"600","margin":"32px 0 16px","color":"#f5f5f5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"The $240 Million Revelation"}]]}],["$","p","201",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"Moonshot's financial disclosure was equally significant: Kimi K2.5 generated $240 million in its first 20 days—exceeding total 2025 revenue. This explosive growth demonstrates the commercial viability of advanced AI models, even as it raises questions about sustainability."}]]}],["$","p","203",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"The dependency on OpenClaw's ecosystem for this surge also highlights a vulnerability: if external platform traffic declines, so does revenue. It's a reminder that in the platform economy, even successful AI companies remain dependent on distribution channels they don't control."}]]}],["$","h2","205",{"style":{"fontSize":"28px","fontWeight":"600","margin":"40px 0 20px","color":"#f5f5f5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"Video Generation: The Next Battlefield"}]]}],["$","h3","207",{"style":{"fontSize":"22px","fontWeight":"600","margin":"32px 0 16px","color":"#f5f5f5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"HappyHorse and the New Competitive Dynamic"}]]}],["$","p","209",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"While ByteDance managed demand through pricing, a mysterious new entrant disrupted video generation rankings. \"HappyHorse\" appeared atop Artificial Analysis's video generation leaderboard on April 9—just as ByteDance implemented price increases."}]]}],["$","p","211",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"The model, developed by Zhang Di (former VP at Kuaishou and technical lead for Kling), achieved an ELO score of 1347—74 points ahead of Seedance 2.0. Zhang returned to Alibaba in November 2025, making HappyHorse an Alibaba project by inference."}]]}],["$","p","213",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"The timing feels strategic: disrupt competitor pricing power by introducing a superior alternative. Whether HappyHorse's lead holds when both models face real-world usage (as opposed to benchmark tests) remains to be seen. But the dynamic illustrates the intensity of video generation competition."}]]}],["$","div","221",{"style":{"overflowX":"auto","margin":"24px 0"},"children":["$","table",null,{"style":{"width":"100%","borderCollapse":"collapse","fontSize":"14px"},"children":[["$","thead",null,{"children":["$","tr",null,{"children":[["$","th","0",{"style":{"borderBottom":"2px solid #22d3ee","padding":"12px 16px","textAlign":"left","fontWeight":"600","color":"#22d3ee"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"Platform"}]]}],["$","th","1",{"style":{"borderBottom":"2px solid #22d3ee","padding":"12px 16px","textAlign":"left","fontW